<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  behavior_tree.xml (problem4)

  Deliberately the SIMPLEST possible reactive-redescend tree this
  theory can express: no obstacle geometry anywhere, exactly ONE
  reactive trigger (battery_below), and ONE fallback with two plain
  branches. Built for the performance investigation into why
  evaluate_plan/4 blows up ProbLog's exact inference once a
  fallback_node's branching gets chained across two or more hops:
  this problem isolates that mechanism from every other variable
  (obstacle count, polygon-restriction geometry, guard style) that
  earlier testing already ruled out one at a time.

  SIMPLIFIED-BRANCH SHAPE: PlanStraight+MoveTo collapse into ONE
  atomic MoveTo(leg_id,algorithm,goal) node, see schema.yaml's own
  MoveToSimplified entry and basic_action_theory.pl's Section 3. No
  more triggers= port: which reactive conditions a leg watches is
  decided by the offline calibrator when it builds that leg's own
  moveto_outcome table, not stated here.

  Fallback:
    Sequence (branch 1: battery allows it):
      BatteryOver threshold=70 : only try the goal leg if battery is
        currently above 70%, checked FRESH each descent (a live state
        check, not history-based), so it correctly fails on its own
        once battery drops below 70% partway through branch 1's walk.
      MoveTo leg_id=leg_try_goal algorithm=straight goal=22.275;2.075
    Sequence (branch 2: go home):
      MoveTo leg_id=leg_go_home algorithm=straight goal=2.275;2.075

  Branch 1's own battery_below(70) trigger is classified `reactive`
  (leg_status/8's catch-all: not completed, not crashed, not
  battery_depleted), so hitting it makes evaluate_plan/4 re-descend
  this WHOLE tree from the halted situation: on that fresh descent,
  branch 1's own BatteryOver threshold=70 guard now reads the CURRENT
  (already-below-70%) battery level and fails immediately, so the
  fallback correctly falls through to branch 2 (go home) on this SAME
  re-descent, without needing any history-based guard (unlike
  problem3's obstacle_on_path, battery level is a simple state fluent
  that's always meaningfully checkable, live, with no "was there ever
  a walk in progress" precondition to work around).

  Start position (2.275,2.075, this problem's own config.yaml) IS the
  "home" this tree returns to; the goal (22.275,2.075) is 20 metres
  east of it, tuned together with config.yaml's raised
  moving_drain_rate (5.0%/time-unit, vs. problem0-3's shared 0.5) so
  the walk crosses the 70% threshold well before reaching the goal.
-->
<root BTCPP_format="4" main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <Fallback name="BatteryFallback">
      <Sequence name="TryGoal">
        <BatteryOver threshold="70"/>
        <MoveTo leg_id="leg_try_goal" algorithm="straight" goal="22.275;2.075"/>
      </Sequence>
      <Sequence name="GoHome">
        <MoveTo leg_id="leg_go_home" algorithm="straight" goal="2.275;2.075"/>
      </Sequence>
    </Fallback>
  </BehaviorTree>

  <TreeNodesModel>
    <Action ID="MoveTo">
      <input_port name="leg_id" type="std::string"/>
      <input_port name="algorithm" type="std::string"/>
      <input_port name="goal" type="Point"/>
    </Action>
    <Condition ID="BatteryOver">
      <input_port name="threshold" type="double"/>
    </Condition>
  </TreeNodesModel>
</root>
